       specificity is set by alternative beta subunits. S. cerevisiae has a single, ATP-specific beta subunit (Lsc2)
       and the purified yeast mitochondrial enzyme uses ATP but not GTP, so the GDP-forming activity is directly
       contradicted for this organism.
+    reason: >-
+      Nucleotide specificity is a property of the beta subunit, not of the alpha subunit that this PANTHER family
+      covers. The nucleotide-binding site lies in the N-terminal ATP-grasp domain of the beta subunit (E. coli
+      photoaffinity labelling and mutagenesis), and in animals the ATP- and GTP-specific isoforms share one alpha
+      subunit and differ only in their beta subunits, which arose by a duplication in early multicellular eukaryotes.
+      The beta-subunit family PTHR11815 already models this correctly: its GDP-forming IBD sits on the GTP-specific
+      beta duplicate clade (PTN000929335), whose members also carry an IKR NOT for the ADP-forming activity, and
+      yeast LSC2 inherits only the ADP-forming term. The GDP-forming IBD on the alpha-family LUCA node PTN000122012
+      therefore rides down into fungi, whose only beta subunit is ATP-specific, and S. cerevisiae has direct
+      biochemical evidence against GTP use. GDP-forming activity is false for the yeast enzyme, so REMOVE; the
+      ADP-forming activity (GO:0004775, contributes_to, IDA/IMP) is the correct and sufficient MF. The fix at source
+      is an IKR NOT for GO:0004776 at the Fungi node of PTHR11117 (PTN000874203), or relocating the alpha-family
+      GDP-forming IBD to nodes whose beta partner is GTP-competent.
     supported_by:
     - reference_id: PMID:9874242
       supporting_text: The yeast succinyl-CoA ligase was shown to utilize ATP but not GTP for succinyl-CoA synthesis
+    - reference_id: PMID:10353839
+      supporting_text: It was concluded that the nucleotide-binding site is located in the N-terminal domain of the beta-subunit.
+    - reference_id: PMID:9765291
+      supporting_text: Highly ATP- and GTP-specific isoforms of succinyl-CoA synthetase in pigeon incorporate the same alpha-subunit, but different beta-subunits
     - reference_id: file:yeast/LSC1/LSC1-uniprot.txt
       record_section: ["CC:CATALYTIC ACTIVITY"]
       supporting_text: Reaction=succinate + ATP + CoA = succinyl-CoA + ADP + phosphate;
